<%# THE SCORER'S PORTRAIT — the face of whoever just scored, filling the space
    the events list vacates. Locals: none. Every value is written by the page's
    script at reveal time.

    IT IS THE SECOND PANE ON THE RAIL'S WHEEL. The frame clips to one pane; this
    fills the one below the events list, and the track rolls up to bring it into
    view. Because the panes are stacked rather than layered, the list and the
    portrait can never be on screen together — which is what an earlier
    cross-fade version got wrong, leaving the scorer's face readable through the
    list of scores.

    ── IT CARRIES NO WORDS, AND THAT IS THE POINT ────────────────────────────
    It used to repeat, beside the picture, the same four lines the status half
    above it was already showing: the city, the action, the play and the player,
    twice in one rail, in two different sizes. The operator's verdict was to
    keep one copy. The words belong ABOVE — that half is a fixed box built to
    hold three or four lines at a readable size — and the face belongs here,
    where it now has the whole pane and can be as large as the rail allows.

    So the two halves divide the announcement rather than duplicating it: what
    happened, then who did it.

    EMPTY AND HIDDEN AT REST. It is painted once per focus tile and stays out of
    the flow until a qualifying score fills it in. That is why it carries no
    server-rendered scorer: the tile is re-rendered by every broadcast, and a
    card holding the LAST score would flash on screen for one frame each time a
    different game scored. %>
<div class="tt-scorer-card h-full w-full flex items-stretch justify-center px-2"
     data-role="scorer-card"
     aria-hidden="true">

  <%# CENTRED, AND THE FULL HEIGHT OF THE PANE.
      ESPN's headshots are transparent cutouts — verified: the corners are
      srgba(0,0,0,0) and the mean alpha survives our resize to five decimals. So
      the player is composited straight onto the team gradient, the way a
      broadcast lower-third does it. An earlier cut put a `bg-surface-alt` circle
      behind the image, which the transparency dutifully revealed as a dark blob;
      the background was ours, not the feed's.

      The source is landscape (600x436), so `object-contain` with an auto width
      shows the whole cutout instead of `object-cover` cropping it into a disc.
      Horizontally centred: with the words gone this pane holds one object, and
      an object alone in a box belongs in the middle of it rather than shoved
      against an edge it no longer shares with anything.

      IT IS BOTTOM-FLUSH AND IT BLEEDS UPWARD. The shoulders sit exactly on the
      card's bottom edge — this pane's floor IS that edge — and the head rises
      past the line where the two team rows meet, into the half that holds the
      words. That is a broadcast lower third, and it is the same move the
      scoring banner's portrait makes against its score line.

      THE BLEED IS BOUGHT BY THE FRAME, NOT BY THIS ELEMENT. The events frame
      clips (the wheel is two panes on a rolling track and depends on it), so no
      amount of height here escapes it. What changed is where the clip line IS:
      the frame keeps half the rail and REACHES UP past the rows' seam by a
      fixed 1.25rem — `h-[calc(50%+1.25rem)] -mt-5` on the tile — so a portrait
      that simply fills it already crosses the divider.

      NOT BY MOVING THE SEAM. An earlier cut gave the frame 62% of the rail,
      which bought the portrait its room out of the WORDS' half and re-centred
      their four lines in a shorter box; the operator's verdict was that the
      centring was right as it was. The calc keeps the floor exact at any tile
      height where a percentage could not — percentage margins resolve against
      the container's WIDTH, and this rail is wider than it is tall.
      LiveFocusSituationRenderTest pins the height and the margin as a pair.

      NOT `transform: scale()`. Growing the drawn image about a bottom origin
      does push the head up, but it pushes the SHOULDERS up too — off the floor
      the operator asked them to sit on — and it scales the cutout's transparent
      padding along with the player. Filling the box keeps the bottom pinned.

      NO CONTAINER QUERY DROPPING IT ANY MORE. The old rules hid the face below
      ~230px of rail because the WORDS beside it were being squeezed to nothing
      and the words won that trade. There are no words here to lose, so a narrow
      rail simply gets a smaller portrait — which is still the whole message
      this pane carries. %>
  <span class="tt-scorer-avatar relative h-full w-full flex items-end justify-center self-stretch">
    <img data-role="scorer-headshot"
         alt=""
         class="h-full w-auto max-w-full object-contain object-bottom hidden">
    <%# No cached headshot: nflverse had no espn_id, or the upload has not run
        for this player. THIS is where a disc belongs — initials need a ground to
        sit on, and there is no cutout here to show one through. The words above
        already name them, so this is a placeholder for a face, not a fallback
        for the information. %>
    <%# The disc stays CENTRED in the pane while the photograph is top-anchored:
        a circle of initials has no head to protect and no shoulders to spend,
        so it simply sits in the middle of the space it is given. %>
    <span data-role="scorer-initials"
          class="w-16 h-16 my-auto self-center rounded-full bg-surface-alt
                 flex items-center justify-center text-lg font-bold text-white/70"></span>
  </span>

</div>
